# This repository is the processor and its command line: the gate-level
# description, the toolchain that builds and proves it, the contracts it runs
# inside, and the netlist they all walk.
#
# The website, the brand assets and the hosting configuration are deliberately
# not published here. They still live in this working tree and still build the
# same way; they are simply not part of what this repository is for.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- dependencies
node_modules/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ secrets
# There are no API keys in this project and no .env to fill in. `npm run
# deploy` reads STEPPER_KEY from the environment, never from a file. These
# lines exist so a stray file can never become a commit.

# A private key never belongs in here either, and the way one gets in is
# always a file made in a hurry with a name nobody thought to ignore. These
# patterns exist so that hurry is survivable.
.deploy-key
*.key
secrets/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --------------------------------------------------------- logs, OS, editor
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generated
# Reproducible outputs. Large, binary or noisy, and all three are the things
# git handles worst.
#   npm run compile → contracts/out/*.json
#   npm run evm     → contracts/out/gas-report.json
#   npm run posters → marketing/out/*.png
contracts/out/
marketing/out/

# ----------------------------------------------------- not part of the chip
# The repository is the processor and its command line. These are the runtime
# and the working notes around it: real files, kept out because they are a
# different job, not because they are secret.
#
# They stay in this file rather than in .git/info/exclude. That file is also
# honoured by the deploy uploader, so a pattern hidden there is a file missing
# from the running image, which is how the site went down once.
.railway/
railway.json
.railwayignore
server.js
tools/serve-check.js
tools/csp-check.js
tools/examples-check.js
docs/domain.md
docs/api-keys.md

# ------------------------------------------------- the site, brand, and copy
# The pages, the stylesheet, the images and the marketing material.
brand/
marketing/
tools/brand/
tools/install-logo.js
tools/render-brand.js
tools/render-figures.js
tools/render-posters.js
tools/render-motion.js
tools/render-video.js
tools/render-stills.js
tools/render-tour.js
tools/score.js
tools/render-audio.js
tools/tour-wallet.js
tools/rpc-relay.js
tools/render-emoji.js
tools/gif.js
tools/mp4.js
tools/verify-terms.js
docs/voice.md

# public/ holds the site, but four of its scripts are the processor itself and
# two more are written by the toolchain. Ignore the directory, then name the
# exceptions. A directory has to be re-admitted before a file inside it can
# be, which is why /public/scripts/ is unignored before its contents.
/public/*
!/public/scripts/
/public/scripts/*
!/public/scripts/st8-data.js
!/public/scripts/machine.js
!/public/scripts/asm.js
!/public/scripts/strategy.js
!/public/scripts/abi.js
!/public/scripts/config.js

# Working instruction, not documentation. Rule 1 explains why.
CLAUDE.md
